\section{Scale convention}{

All estimates enter and leave the package on the \strong{log scale} for
multiplicative effect measures (HR, RR, OR): pass \code{log(1.02)}, not
\code{1.02}. The package never log-transforms for you; \code{effect_measure}
only labels output. Plots can back-transform via \code{scale = "natural"}.
}

\section{The comparisons table (input)}{

One row per estimated drug-versus-drug effect (the same pair may
appear in any number of rows). Required columns:
\describe{
\item{\code{study_id}}{Character (or coercible). Identifier of the study
or analysis the estimate came from. Repeats are allowed.}
\item{\code{target}}{Character. The drug the estimate is \emph{for} (the
numerator of a hazard ratio).}
\item{\code{comparator}}{Character. The drug compared against (the
denominator). Must differ from \code{target} on every row.}
\item{\code{estimate}}{Numeric, no missing values. The estimated effect
of \code{target} versus \code{comparator} on the log scale.}
\item{\code{std_error}}{Numeric, strictly positive on every row. The
standard error of \code{estimate} on the log scale.}
}
Any additional column is preserved untouched through the pipeline.
The estimand-describing columns — \code{population}, \code{time_at_risk},
\code{outcome_definition}, \code{database}, \code{study_design}, \code{design},
\code{outcome}, \code{estimand} — additionally trigger a warning at
construction when their values differ across comparisons, because
statistical connectivity does not establish causal transportability.

Construction fails loudly on: a missing required column, zero rows,
a self-comparison (\code{target == comparator}), a missing or empty drug
name, a missing estimate, or a non-positive standard error.
}

\section{The anchors table (input)}{

Optional. One row per absolute (placebo-controlled) estimate:
\describe{
\item{\code{study_id}}{Character (or coercible). Trial identifier.}
\item{\code{drug}}{Character. The anchored drug. Must appear in at
least one comparison — a drug appearing only in anchors is
rejected as a likely data error.}
\item{\code{reference}}{Character, normally \code{"placebo"}. What the drug
was compared against; defines the zero of the absolute scale.}
\item{\code{estimate}}{Numeric, no missing values. The estimated effect
of \code{drug} versus \code{reference} on the log scale.}
\item{\code{std_error}}{Numeric, strictly positive. Its standard error.}
}
